     1→\begin{abstract}
     2→\deleted{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) estimation is critical for climate monitoring, hydrological modeling, and water resource management, yet current approaches face fundamental limitations. Optical sensors provide rich spectral information but are severely limited by cloud contamination, while Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) offers all-weather capability but lacks spectral richness for precise snow characterization. Existing multi-sensor fusion approaches either employ simplistic strategies like concatenation or ensemble that fail to capture complex inter-modal relationships, or ignore the inherent multi-resolution nature of operational satellite missions, limiting their ability to generate high-resolution predictions from coarse-resolution training labels. This paper introduces a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) to generate high-resolution fractional snow cover maps at 10m spatial resolution. The architecture addresses multi-resolution challenges through a novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy: S1/S2 features are fused at 10m through bidirectional cross-attention, aggregated to 60m for supervised training against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) reference data, and disaggregated back to 10m through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail. Dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for SAR, ECA for optical) extract hierarchical features, cross-attention fusion captures inter-sensor dependencies through explicit query-key-value transformations, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) model global context. Quality-aware training incorporates multiplicative loss weighting based on multi-source metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) with curriculum learning, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Comprehensive evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test with 30-day temporal separation, 18,117 test patches from 4 unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirms strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$; $R^2=0.588$ explaining 58.8\% variance) and physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$), substantially exceeding baselines (CNN-Only: $R^2=0.239$; Early Fusion: $R^2=0.026$). Systematic ablation studies validate each component's contribution: removing cross-attention causes 5.16 point accuracy drop, removing transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points. Critically, uncertainty quantification demonstrates excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 $<$ 0.03 threshold, mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching the observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns with elevated values at snow-land boundaries (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$). Test-time augmentation improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. The methodological contributions in multi-resolution training, cross-modal attention fusion, quality-aware learning, have broad implications for earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data with quantified uncertainty.}
     3→
     4→\added{Accurate fractional snow cover (FSC) mapping requires combining the complementary strengths of optical imagery (spectral discrimination) and Synthetic Aperture Radar (all-weather capability), yet existing fusion approaches employ simplistic concatenation strategies that fail to model complex inter-modal relationships, while ignoring the resolution mismatch between high-resolution inputs and coarse reference labels. We introduce XFuse, a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that addresses these challenges through three key contributions: (1) \textbf{bidirectional cross-attention fusion} that explicitly models SAR-optical interdependencies via query-key-value transformations, enabling each modality to selectively attend to complementary information from the other; (2) a \textbf{multi-resolution training strategy} ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) that fuses Sentinel-1/2 features at native resolution, aggregates to 60m for supervision against Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) labels, and disaggregates through guided upsampling for enhanced spatial detail; and (3) \textbf{quality-aware curriculum learning} that incorporates multi-source metadata (cloud masks, atmospheric parameters, QC flags) through adaptive loss weighting, enabling robust training from heterogeneous satellite data while providing calibrated uncertainty estimates. Evaluation on 29 Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland (18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles) demonstrates state-of-the-art performance: 13.77\% MAE and 94.66\% classification accuracy, representing 38.7\% MAE reduction over the best baseline. Ablation studies confirm cross-attention as the largest individual contributor (5.16 percentage point accuracy gain), while uncertainty quantification achieves excellent calibration (ECE=0.025). The methodology generalizes to earth observation applications requiring fusion of heterogeneous multi-resolution satellite data.}
     5→
     6→    \textbf{Keywords:} Fractional snow cover, multi-modal fusion, hybrid CNN-Transformer, cross-attention, multi-resolution processing, quality-aware learning, uncertainty quantification, Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2
     7→    \end{abstract}

