     1→\section{Methodology}
     2→\label{sec:proposed_method}
     3→
     4→\subsection{Overview}
     5→
     6→Our methodology addresses the challenge of high-resolution fractional snow cover estimation through a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that fuses Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m). The framework integrates three principal innovations: multi-resolution processing that enables training at 60m resolution while producing 10m predictions, cross-attention fusion that explicitly models inter-sensor dependencies, and quality-aware learning that leverages auxiliary metadata for robust training under heterogeneous data conditions. Figure~\ref{fig:overall_methodology} illustrates the complete processing pipeline from satellite data acquisition through preprocessing, multi-modal fusion, and high-resolution prediction.
     7→
     8→\added{\textbf{Distinction from Prior Work.} While SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training have been explored independently in remote sensing literature, existing approaches face specific limitations that our methodology addresses. First, conventional fusion strategies (concatenation, late fusion, attention-weighted averaging) treat modalities symmetrically without explicitly modeling \textit{how} information from one sensor should inform interpretation of the other; our bidirectional cross-attention enables SAR features to selectively query relevant optical context and vice versa through learned query-key-value projections (Section~\ref{sec:cross_attention}). Second, multi-resolution approaches typically employ fixed aggregation (average/max pooling) that ignores scene content; our adaptive aggregation learns to preserve snow-relevant information through content-aware pooling decisions (Section~\ref{sec:multi_resolution}). Third, existing quality-aware methods often use binary masks or single-source quality indicators; our framework integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}). The novelty lies not in the individual concepts but in their principled integration within a unified architecture designed for the specific challenges of operational satellite-based snow monitoring.}
